#!/usr/bin/ruby
# frozen_string_literal: true

# harness-gate — run the whole shipwright gate in one shot, in the verified-
# correct invocation form for each check.
#
# Why this exists: the gate used to live ONLY as prose in
# ai/agents/athena-shipwright.md.in -> "The gate". Prose is not executable, so
# every run re-assembled the nine commands by hand, and a mis-assembly is
# dangerous in ONE specific direction: a check that silently does nothing still
# exits 0 and reads as PASS. That has already happened once — invoking a hook as
# `ai/hooks/<hook>.sh --self-test` ignores the flag and blocks on (or empties)
# stdin, a FALSE GREEN. The template now warns about it in prose; this script
# makes the correct form the only form, and its --self-test asserts the trap
# cannot creep back into the declared list.
#
# This runner is a convenience + a guard, NOT a new source of authority: the
# canonical, human-readable gate spec stays in the shipwright template. Keep the
# two in sync — add a check here AND there.
#
# Deliberately gem-free (stdlib only); run with bare `ruby`.
#
# Usage:
#   ai/bin/harness-gate              run every check; exit 1 if any fails
#   ai/bin/harness-gate --jobs N     the same, on N workers
#   ai/bin/harness-gate --list       print the declared checks, run nothing
#   ai/bin/harness-gate --self-test  verify the runner's own logic (every group)
#   ai/bin/harness-gate --self-test --group NAME   one group (DND-1007)
#
# Workers (DND-1005): `--jobs N` wins over the HARNESS_GATE_JOBS environment
# variable; with neither, half the cores, at least 1 and at most 8. Both must
# be a positive integer: anything else is exit 2 with a Fix:, before any check
# runs. `--jobs 1` is the old serial run. --jobs applies only to a gate run.
#
# Per-check timings (DND-1005). Every output line carries the check's wall
# and CPU seconds. Every run also appends one JSON object per check to
#   $XDG_STATE_HOME/athena/harness-gate/timings.jsonl
#   (~/.local/state/athena/harness-gate/timings.jsonl when XDG_STATE_HOME is
#   unset or empty)
# with these keys:
#   at      UTC time the run's results were recorded, ISO 8601
#   tree    the directory the checks ran in (the gated worktree)
#   head    that tree's HEAD SHA ("" when it cannot be read)
#   label   the check's label, as in --list
#   wall_s  wall seconds, from spawn to reap (2 decimals)
#   cpu_s   user+system CPU seconds of the check's process tree (3
#           decimals), or null when it could not be measured; see
#           CPU SECONDS PER CHECK below for what the tree includes
#   ok      true when the check passed
#   jobs    the worker count of that run
# The file is capped (TIMINGS_MAX_ROWS, trimmed to the newest
# TIMINGS_KEEP_ROWS). The next run reads it back: the last wall_s per label
# orders the pool longest-first. A failed write is a NOTE, never a verdict.
# An interrupted run (see INTERRUPTED RUNS below) records nothing.
#
# SCRATCH-HOME SENTINEL (DND-1316). Every check runs with a fresh sentinel
# directory first on PATH (ai/lib/scratch_home_sentinel.rb). The watched tools
# are what asdf shims here (ruby, node, mix, ...) plus every name in a PATH
# `shims` directory. A check that runs one through PATH while HOME is not the
# gate's own HOME FAILS. The report names the tool and the HOME. The check's
# own exit status does not matter. In an agent session that tool is an asdf
# shim, which exits 126 under a scratch HOME (DND-1203). The sentinel execs
# the next tool on PATH, so a check that keeps its PATH behaves as it would
# without it. It fires on any machine, lane or agent session. A check that
# deletes its sentinel, or a gate HOME that is not absolute, fails the check as
# "could not measure", never as clean.
#
# Not in the list: the gate RUN itself (it would recurse). Its --self-test IS
# declared (DND-507): it never runs the gate, only `--list` and fixtures.

require "open3"
require "rbconfig"
require "fileutils"
require "tmpdir"
require "stringio"
require "json"
require "etc"
require "shellwords"
require "digest"
require_relative "../lib/first_party"
require_relative "../lib/landed"
require_relative "../lib/reap_tags"
require_relative "../lib/scratch_home_sentinel"
require "securerandom"
